# er-spack-development: Singularity + Spack commands

This file is generated by `setup.py`.

## Inputs used by setup.py

- Spack version: `{{ spack_version }}` (container image: `{{ sif_path }}`)
- `SPACK_USER_CONFIG_PATH`: `{{ spack_user_config_path }}`
- Environment name: `{{ environment_name }}` (manifest: `{{ env_manifest_path }}`)
- er-spack-packages repo: `{{ repo_dir }}` (branch recorded at setup: `{{ packages_branch }}`)
- Environments dir: `{{ envs_dir }}`
- Outputs dir: `{{ outputs_dir }}` (this environment installs to `{{ env_install_root }}/apps` and `{{ env_install_root }}/modules`)

## Batch steps

Each script submits one Slurm job (Singularity runs on compute nodes only) and
defaults to the environment recorded at setup:

```bash
./spack_concretize.py                  # spack -e {{ environment_name }} concretize -f
./spack_pkgs_install.py                # spack -e {{ environment_name }} install
./spack_mod_refresh.py                 # spack -e {{ environment_name }} module tcl refresh -y
```

Use `-e <env-name>` for another environment, `--constraint zen3` when the
environment pins a target, and `--wait` to chain the steps in order:

```bash
./spack_concretize.py --wait && ./spack_pkgs_install.py --wait && ./spack_mod_refresh.py
```

Job scripts, logs, and build-result YAMLs land in `{{ build_results_dir }}`.
`--dry-run` prints the job script instead of submitting.

## Start a Singularity shell

`singularity` is only on compute nodes: start an interactive session first, then
shell into the container with the same binds as the batch jobs:

```bash
srun -p interruptible_cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=4 --cpus-per-task=1 \
     --mem-per-cpu=4096 --pty /bin/bash -l

export SPACK_USER_CONFIG_PATH={{ spack_user_config_path }}

singularity shell \
  --env SPACK_USER_CONFIG_PATH="${SPACK_USER_CONFIG_PATH}" \
  --bind {{ script_dir }}:{{ script_dir }} \
  --bind {{ repo_dir }}:{{ repo_dir }} \
  --bind {{ envs_dir }}:{{ envs_dir }} \
  --bind {{ outputs_dir }}:{{ outputs_dir }} \
  --bind {{ spack_user_config_path }}:{{ spack_user_config_path }} \
  {{ sif_path }}
```

## Manage the environment inside the shell

```bash
source /opt/spack/share/spack/setup-env.sh   # needed for env activate/deactivate

spack env list
spack -e {{ environment_name }} env status
spack -e {{ environment_name }} add 'zlib@1.3' # or edit {{ env_manifest_path }}
spack -e {{ environment_name }} spec           # preview the solve before committing to it
spack -e {{ environment_name }} concretize -f
spack -e {{ environment_name }} install
spack -e {{ environment_name }} module tcl refresh -y
spack -e {{ environment_name }} find

spack env activate {{ environment_name }}       # add -p to show [{{ environment_name }}] in the prompt
spack env deactivate

spack repo list                                # which er-spack-packages checkout is in scope
spack config get repos
```

## Use the modules

```bash
export MODULEPATH={{ env_install_root }}/modules:${MODULEPATH}
module avail
```
